# TRAC project ignores

# Data (large)
data/
../trac_data/

# Experiments outputs. Checkpoints and run logs ARE large, so they stay ignored --
# at ANY depth: the old `experiments/*/...` form matched one level only and missed
# experiments/phaseX/expY/training_log.txt -> bloated commits.
experiments/**/checkpoint.pt
**/checkpoint.pt.safe
**/training_log.txt

# Result tables (.csv) under experiments/ are DELIVERABLES: they are small and
# carry params/FLOPs/per-task metrics/git-commit for every run. Without them a
# phase is not reproducible, so they are tracked BY DEFAULT -- there is
# deliberately NO ignore rule for them.
#
# History: this used to be `experiments/*/*.csv` (ignore) + a hand-maintained
# `!whitelist`. That shape failed three separate ways, all silently:
#   * phase6 was never added to the whitelist -> every Phase-6 table (registry /
#     hypotheses / round-2 statistics) stayed untracked for days;
#   * phase2b's `r1r2_paramaudit.csv` does not end in `_results.csv`, so the
#     narrow `!experiments/phase2b/*_results.csv` form never matched it;
#   * widening the ignore's reach reopened the hole for any non-`phase*` top-level
#     directory -- verified by planting experiments/zz_probe_tmp/probe.csv, which
#     `experiments/*/*.csv` swallowed even with the `!phase*/**` whitelist present.
#     `experiments/_figures/` and `experiments/_template/` are exactly such dirs.
# The lesson is the same one as the kd_cache incident below: adding or renaming a
# directory must never require remembering to edit this file.
# Guarded by check_repo_hygiene.sh assertion A1b ("no .csv under experiments/ may
# be ignored"); oversized adds are separately caught by A2 (>= 1 MB).

# Baseline repos keep their own .git; ignore their weight files
baselines/*/pretrained/
baselines/*/weights/
*.pth
*.pt
*.onnx
*.safetensors

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/

# IDE / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
# The 2026-09-10 Phase-1B/2B/2C directory reorganisation moved the KD feature
# cache to experiments/phase2b/expF_single_teacher/kd_cache/. The two literal
# rules that used to sit here (`experiments/*/kd_cache/` and
# `experiments/expF_single_teacher/kd_cache/`) matched NEITHER the new path, so
# 21 GB of *.npy was one `git add -A` away from being committed. Match at ANY
# depth, and keep NO literal path rules in this file: they rot silently on the
# next rename (check_repo_hygiene.sh assertion A4 exists to catch that).
experiments/**/kd_cache/

# Baseline/teacher weights and key trained models (uploaded for the server).
# NOTE: weights/YOLOP_End-to-end.pth is 95.7 MB -- 4.3 MB below GitHub's 100 MB
# hard limit, and ~83% of the packed repo. It is REGISTERED as an allowed large
# blob in scripts/hygiene_exceptions.txt (assertion A3), so the exception is
# auditable rather than implicit.
!weights/
!trained_models/
!weights/*.pth
!trained_models/*.pt

# Verbose run-log captures (stdout of train/eval/launcher, NOT deliverable data).
# Keep eval_stdout.log (small, provenance of reported metric) + metrics.json + config.yaml.
**/train_stdout.log
**/*_foreground.log
**/*_runner.log
**/*_monitor.log
**/*_launch.log
**/zsweep_*.log
**/r1r2_*.log
**/expA_*.log
**/expD_*.log
**/exp4C_*.log
**/*_diag.log
**/status.txt

# Ignore ALL run/verbose logs except the eval provenance log (deliverable).
# This catches ad-hoc logs (ours_*.log, dyn*.log, finish.log, *_eval.log, ...)
# that the specific patterns below might miss.
**/*.log
!**/eval_stdout.log

# baseline 公共仓库内容由服务器自行 clone，不纳入本仓库
baselines/

# Large derived cache / artifacts (never commit; GH001 rejects >100MB files)
*.npz
experiments/**/*.partial_*
experiments/**/clean_master_chain.log

data/bdd100k/official_eval/
experiments/phase6/official_gt_audit/
.commit_msg_*.txt
experiments/phase6/**/*.lock
experiments/phase6/**/.d8_seeds.lock
experiments/phase6/final/ABORTS.txt
experiments/phase6/final/STOP_CHAIN
# Runtime control files (CORRECTED 2026-09-14). Both are created and removed by the
# chains themselves, so "absent right now" is their NORMAL state -- not a rename
# leftover. They must stay ignored: a committed STOP_CHAIN would halt the very next
# runner start (scripts/phase6_final100_run.sh:37 -- it IS the polite abort lever, see
# scripts/_make_final_runner.py:50 "Keep STOP_CHAIN (harmless, and it is our abort
# lever)"), and ABORTS.txt is a per-run abort marker. The rule was removed earlier
# today on a WRONG reading (the grep was truncated by `head -20`, so the runner's own
# use at :26/:37 never appeared); restored here with the matching audit exemption
# (RUNTIME_EXEMPT in scripts/audit_gitignore.py), which is the correct fix -- the audit
# targets rename leftovers, not runtime markers.
# Generated single-file report (2026-09-14). The HTML is a BUILD PRODUCT of
# docs/PHASE6_TECHNICAL_REPORT.md + experiments/phase6/figures/*.png, with every PNG
# inlined as base64 so the file stands alone for a reader with no network. That makes
# it 3.4 MB, over the 1 MB A2 budget, and it is reproducible byte-for-byte by
# `python scripts/phase6_build_report_html.py`. So the SOURCE is committed (the .md,
# the figure PNGs and both scripts) and the bundle is not -- same logic as
# checkpoint.pt. Regenerate, do not commit.
docs/PHASE6_TECHNICAL_REPORT.html

# process logs are never deliverables
*.log

# baseline pretrained weights: fetched from upstream repos, never committed
weights/
